@description REAPER MCP — Model Context Protocol interface (immersive/spatial audio)
@version 1.17.0
@author James Livingston
@link https://github.com/inseglet-audio/inseglet
@provides
  [darwin] reaper_mcp.dylib https://github.com/inseglet-audio/inseglet/releases/download/v$version/reaper_mcp.dylib
@changelog
  The true-peak meter now uses the interpolation filter ITU-R BS.1770-4 publishes, and keeps the sample peak as a floor.

  `analysis.meter`'s per-channel `truePeakDb` -- and the mid/side and downmix reports that share it --
  oversample 4x, as BS.1770-4 Annex 2 describes. Until now the interpolator was a short Hann-windowed
  sinc. It reads inside the +0.2/-0.4 dB tolerance EBU Tech 3341 sets on every one of its true-peak
  test signals, so nothing you measured was out of tolerance -- but it droops in the top octave, and on
  dense, bright material it recovered less of the inter-sample peak than the Recommendation's own
  filter does: about 0.15 dB less at 44.1 kHz on a nine-partial test chord, and always low.

  WHAT CHANGES. The interpolator is now the 48-tap, 4-phase filter tabulated in BS.1770-4 Annex 2,
  coefficient for coefficient, so the reading agrees with meters built on that table. The input sample
  itself is kept as a floor, because the table alone can read a sample-aligned peak up to 0.2 dB below
  the sample, and a true peak below the sample peak is not a reading anyone should act on.

  What you will notice: on bright, dense masters `truePeakDb` reads a little higher than before -- a
  few hundredths to about 0.15 dB -- and it is the more accurate figure. On a steady tone the reading
  can move either way by up to about 0.3 dB depending on the frequency and on where the peak falls
  between samples: up near 0.4x the sample rate, where the old filter was weakest, and down around a
  sixth of the sample rate, where the Recommendation's filter has its own small dip. Both are inside
  the published tolerance. The deliverable gate (`analysis.check_deliverable`) is unchanged -- it has
  always used REAPER's own true-peak engine.

  The unit tests now include Tech 3341's five true-peak signals at the published tolerance, so the
  meter's conformance is tested on every build rather than assumed.

  No tools were added or removed.
